{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791284584,"nonce":"99VesHNyjlS5G8y8pqVDIe8KTBU=","title":"Enum pattern results lose concrete generic field types","message":"Two independent repros surfaced while normalizing constructor payloads. With a correct native payload in Option\u003cInt\u003e, a switch expression returning the bound field is still emitted as a *void phi. Passing a negative result to an Int parameter can print -10 != -10 in the default tiered interpreter because the pointer form and the declared Int disagree. Calling an Int consumer directly within the case works.\n\nRepro:\nimport haxe.ds.Option;\nclass Main {\n    static function compareInt(a:Int, b:Int) { if (a != b) throw a + \" != \" + b; }\n    static function main() {\n        var input:Dynamic = -10;\n        var value:Option\u003cInt\u003e = Some(input);\n        compareInt(switch value { case Some(n): n; case _: 0; }, -10);\n    }\n}\n\nA nested generic pattern also loses Float: with Option\u003cOption\u003cFloat\u003e\u003e initialized as Some(Some(dynamicFloat)), switching the outer value and then switching the bound inner value yields the Float bits as an integer (4609434218613702656 instead of 1.5). Passing inner to a declared Option\u003cFloat\u003e consumer before matching preserves the Float type.\n\nObserved with --release --no-cache --preset application after enum payload conversion. The concrete fields are stored correctly; the remaining issue is preserving the generic field type in pattern-bound expression typing and joins. These are separate from converting Dynamic arguments at construction.","files":null}]}